    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d636">

                <pb facs="0860" n="848" sameAs="frus:frus1945v05/pg848"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 E.W./5–845: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Kennan</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-05-08T13:00:00+03:00">May 8, 1945—1
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received May 8—7:50 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1496. Role of Allied armies in final stages of war continues to receive most
                    inconspicuous treatment in Moscow press whereas role of Red Army is prominently
                    featured. For the past week there has been no more than passing editorial
                    reference to military achievements of Allied armies in the flood of articles
                    concerning fall of Berlin and other Red Army triumphs.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Kennan</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
